A||||17||Lance||Kill switch?||||5||
Z||000000||Lance||02-27-2000||03:28 AM||||I have herd that people love to take other peoples Fieros. I want to put a kill switch in mine. What would I hook it up to, the battery or the ignition? If the ignition, what wire. By the way, it’s an 85 Sports coupe.<P>Thanx<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||02-27-2000||06:21 PM||||I have run a kill to the fuel pump through my alarm... the ignition is killed also all controlled via an Alpine SEA-8081 (alarm) I also have my computer hard wired into a switch behind the speed-o pod thingy... the 2 red wires that supply power to the ECU are cut and spliced in to this switch... my computer has memory issues and i have to clear them every once in a while... (not originaly intended to be theft deterent but it works)  (or annoying friend that thinks he can drive your car with out you knowing deterent) ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||02-27-2000||07:26 PM||||Does this reset the ECM everytime you use this? I imagine that wouldn't be good?!||||reg||5
Z||000003||fiero56||02-27-2000||07:35 PM||||I think he means the ECM switch is separate from the alarm. Probably just a flipper switch under the dash somewhere.<BR>I would like to know how to wire a F/P relay into my car alarm though. I have had a separate stand alone switch in mind for a while. Maybe I can have it wired into my alarm.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||02-27-2000||07:51 PM||||Its run just like your ignition kill it stops the ground from being made thus no power to the relay-fuel pump-no gas-no theft! I only use the switch when i'm at the track or running it REALLY hard with the nitrous... my O2 flakes and messes with the performance. With the custom chip and different injectors it kicks itself into a type of Limp-home mode after a lengthy run... flip the switch let it roll for 10 of 15 seconds flip the switch back on pop the clutch.. I don't even miss the next gimp in a lame-o S/C civic... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierosound||02-27-2000||08:25 PM||||Some shop installed akill switch in my friend's van.  The CIGARETTE LIGHTER complete a switch for the relay for the starter.  If you don't push in the cigarette lighter first, IT WON"T start.  The lighter works as normal.  Also, when the lighter is not pressed in, and you accidentally turn the key - you don't get that god awful sound of the starter gears grinding on the flywheel! Cool!!||||reg||10
Z||000006||V6pack||02-27-2000||08:47 PM||||Has anyone here tried the new Diehard anti-theft device battery with the "alarm like" keychain remote that disables the battery for starts until the remote is pushed.<P>I assume that there is enough low current flow to operate clocks, after shut off fans, and to keep radios from defaulting to 87.5...:-))<P>It sounds like a good solution.||||reg||10
Z||000007||Cooter||02-27-2000||09:06 PM||||you could just unplug the fuel pump fuse under the dash. A switch could also be added in there with an inline fuse. If you expect to have trouble, pull the coil wire and swap in on the distributer with a plug wire. A thief will try to crank the car, but only one cylinder will fire. Someone would really have to be looking to find the switched wires.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||02-27-2000||09:25 PM||||When I park downtown I always pull the coil/dist wire, and also arm my alarm. Since I don't have power decklid release, it would take some screwing around with the car, and I always park near a security post in the parking garages. The guards are not always there, but I feel better parking there.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||02-27-2000||10:02 PM||||Lance,<BR>My advice is to buy an alarm system and install it yourself. They are not hard. Just one thing "Do Not" put the brain box on the left hand side under the dash. This is where the Alarm Installers book says to put it. If it's in a book then the car thief also knows where to look. Try to put the brain box where he would never look or where he cannot get to it easily. Also always wrap wires with the same kind of tape as factory harness' or even better remove tabe from factory harness add alarm wires and rewrap the entire harness. A thief won't spend more that 30 - 60 seconds trying to get the car. Time is not on his side. I have installed many alarm systems and have helped catch a few car thieves (amatures)with my installs.||||reg||1
Z||000010||daved||02-27-2000||10:25 PM||||I always thought it better to discourage the thief before he made an attempt to break into the car....so I bought one of those heavy duty "crook" things for my steering wheel to visually discourage any attempt.  Under the theory that its too late to worry about the alarm once the thief is insde the car, the damage has already begun. Has anyone had their Fiero stolen with one of these devices installed?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||02-27-2000||11:21 PM||||The "Club" type of steering wheel lock is a good visual deterant. And most Thieves won't bother wasting time. BUT the "Club" type devices advertise it would take 3 to 5 minutes to cut thru the device. They don't tell you is if the thief wants your car bad enough it only takes 28 seconds to cut the steering wheel! For visual deterance I suggest a few things. The club, a very bright LED for the alarm in a easily seen place. No decals as to the type of alarm system (so they don't know the brand name, and Finally A very large Rotwiller Dog!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||02-28-2000||12:16 AM||||Another tip- Always lock the car down with the parking brake. A lot of time, people not familair with the Fiero's handle lift and release will not be be able to figure it out quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||02-28-2000||06:06 AM||||Sometimes even Fiero owners have no idea how the parking brake works!  Mine has never worked so I never messed with it.  I rode with WT III on the 2/5 Ohio trip and saw how it worked for the 1st time.  I felt kinda stupid, but my car is an auto so I never bothered with it.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Lance||02-28-2000||06:39 PM||||Is there a trick to useing the emergency brake? I cant seem to get it to come up. I have pushed the butten and tried to pull, but it acts lice its stuck. Please bare with me, This is my first Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>Thanx for the help all.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Lance (edited 02-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Oreif||02-29-2000||01:40 AM||||Lance, DON'T force the E-brake! The cables are most likely frozen. If you force it the back brakes will be locked on. You need to replace the brake cables. This is common on Fieros especially Automatics due to the parking brake isn't used. If you change the cables I suggest using the E-brakes all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Lance||02-29-2000||03:06 AM||||Thanx Oreif. I haven't forced becouse I thought I might break something. Does anyone know where I can get a new one? Or am I going to have to get a used one off a parts car?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Bill Osmer||02-29-2000||06:06 PM||||Lance -<BR>Don't waste your money on used cables!!! Most likely, you'll need one cable (what's called the "front cable"). It's the one that connects from the e-brake hadle to the equalizers at the rear of the Fiero. Go to your friendly Pontiac dealer and order a new one - less than $20. To remove the old one may require grinding/cutting the old one - do this in the driver's rear wheel well, and the job will be easier.||||reg||14
